      "dom_id": "question-9909",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $y=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $u=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-{sin}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since ${sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
