      "dom_id": "question-17921",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\frac{1}{x})}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
